              %path{:d => "M 6.382667 55.126185 L 79.101282 -52.48859 ", :style => "fill:none;stroke-width:0.47818;stroke-linecap:butt;stroke-linejoin:miter;stroke:rgb(0%,0%,0%);stroke-opacity:1;stroke-miterlimit:10;", :transform => "matrix(0.997155,0,0,-0.997155,100.979243,76.30528)"}
              %path{:d => "M -2.486381 2.870674 C -2.034312 1.146786 -1.019157 0.333142 -0.000995654 -0.000276415 C -1.020067 -0.332893 -2.033459 -1.146877 -2.487538 -2.868352 ", :style => "fill:none;stroke-width:0.47818;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round;stroke:rgb(0%,0%,0%);stroke-opacity:1;stroke-miterlimit:10;", :transform => "matrix(0.558297,0.826193,0.826193,-0.558297,179.989065,128.840512)"}
              %g{:style => "fill:rgb(0%,0%,0%);fill-opacity:1;"}
                %use{:x => "147.238253", "xlink:href" => "#c6U1fYEwyIC1hDInenl5zvPsk7E=-glyph3-2", :y => "69.749984"}
              %path{:d => "M -73.716321 -65.0791 L 73.240858 -65.0791 ", :style => "fill:none;stroke-width:0.47818;stroke-linecap:butt;stroke-linejoin:miter;stroke:rgb(0%,0%,0%);stroke-opacity:1;stroke-miterlimit:10;", :transform => "matrix(0.997155,0,0,-0.997155,100.979243,76.30528)"}
              %path{:d => "M -2.486257 2.869611 C -2.031839 1.145957 -1.021151 0.335056 0.00128935 -0.00183956 C -1.021151 -0.334818 -2.031839 -1.145719 -2.486257 -2.869373 ", :style => "fill:none;stroke-width:0.47818;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round;stroke:rgb(0%,0%,0%);stroke-opacity:1;stroke-miterlimit:10;", :transform => "matrix(0.997155,0,0,-0.997155,174.248714,141.197384)"}
              %g{:style => "fill:rgb(0%,0%,0%);fill-opacity:1;"}
                %use{:x => "98.445472", "xlink:href" => "#c6U1fYEwyIC1hDInenl5zvPsk7E=-glyph3-3", :y => "148.979919"}
              %g{:style => "fill:rgb(0%,0%,0%);fill-opacity:1;"}
                %use{:x => "99.323966", "xlink:href" => "#c6U1fYEwyIC1hDInenl5zvPsk7E=-glyph4-1", :y => "144.921498"}
          %p Finally, the 2-cells can be composed in a more or less evident way, akin to composition in a multicategory:
          %p
            %img{:alt => "A composition in a virtual double category", :src => "/nlab/files/virtual-double-category-composite.png"}/
          %p
            Virtual double categories are related to double categories precisely as ordinary multicategories are related to monoidal categories (see
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/generalized+multicategory"} generalized multicategory
            and
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tensor+product"} tensor product
          %p
            The dual notion in which 2-cells have a single loose source and a multi-ary loose target may be called a covirtual double category, although the term oplax double category also exists in the literature e.g.
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#DawsonParePronk10"} Dawson, Paré, Pronk
          %h2#definition Definition
          %p A virtual double category can be defined in two equivalent ways:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                It is a
                = succeed "-" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi T
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/generalized+multicategory"} multicategory
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi T
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
                on
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quiver"} directed graphs
                whose algebras are categories. For this reason, Leinster originally called them
                = succeed "," do
                  %strong fc-multicategories
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi T
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
                which stands for “free-category.”
            %li
              %p
                It is a generalized multicategory, in the sense of Hermida, Cruttwell-Shulman, and others, relative to the monad
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi T
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
                on graphs-internal-to-Cat whose algebras are double categories. This is the origin of the name “virtual double category,” in line with the general terminology “virtual
                = succeed "-algebra\u201D" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi T
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
          %p We can also give an explicit definition, which was more or less already given in the “Idea” section: all that is missing are identities and associativity for 2-cell composition.
          %h2#examples Examples
          %ul
            %li
              A
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/multicategory"} multicategory
              is a virtual double category with a single object and a single tight morphism (the identity morphism).
            %li Any double category is an example, and thus also any bicategory viewing the arrows as loose.
            %li
              For any
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoidal+category"} monoidal category
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi V
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
              = succeed "-matrices" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi V
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi p
                    %mo :
                    %mi X
                    %mo →
                    %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p : X \to Y
              is a family of objects
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi p
                      %mrow
                        %mi y
                        %mo ,
                        %mi x
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi V
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    p_{y,x}
                    \\in V
              for each
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi X
                      %mo ,
                      %mi y
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi Y
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x\in X, y \in Y
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi X
                      %mn 0
                    %mover
                      %mo →
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi p
                          %mn 1
                    %msub
                      %mi X
                      %mn 1
                    %mo →
                    %mi …
                    %mo →
                    %msub
                      %mi X
                      %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    X_0
                    \\overset{p_1}{\to} X_1 \to\dots \to X_n
              to
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi Y
                      %mn 0
                    %mover
                      %mo →
                      %mi q
                    %msub
                      %mi Y
                      %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y_0 \overset{q}{\to} Y_1
              along
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo :
                    %msub
                      %mi X
                      %mn 0
                    %mo →
                    %msub
                      %mi Y
                      %mn 0
                    %mo ,
                    %mi g
                    %mo :
                    %msub
                      %mi X
                      %mi n
                    %mo →
                    %msub
                      %mi Y
                      %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f : X_0 \to Y_0, g : X_n \to Y_n
              is a family of arrows
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi α
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi x
                          %mn 0
                        %mo ,
                        %mo .
                        %mo .
                        %mo .
                    %mo :
                    %msub
                      %mi p
                      %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi x
                      %mn 1
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi x
                      %mn 0
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ⊗
                    %msub
                      %mi p
                      %mn 2
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi x
                      %mn 2
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi x
                      %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ⊗
                    %mi …
                    %mo →
                    %mi q
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi g
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi x
                      %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ,
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi x
                      %mn 0
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\alpha_{x_0,...} : p_1(x_1,x_0)\otimes p_2(x_2,x_1)\otimes\dots \to
                    q(g(x_n),f(x_0))
              in
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi V
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
              (using the unit of the monoidal category if the source string is empty). If
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi V
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
              has certain colimits that are preserved by
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ⊗
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \otimes
              then composites exist and this virtual double category is pseudo.
            %li
              Given a monad on a virtual double category, the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/generalized+multicategory"} loose kleisli double category
              produces a virtual double category that is only pseudo under strong conditions on the monad. In particular, “free monoid” monad on the double category of sets and spans does not produce a pseudo double category.
          %h2#higher_categories_of_virtual_double_categories Higher categories of virtual double categories
          %p
            There are notions of
            = succeed "," do
              %strong functor
            = succeed "," do
              %strong transformation
            %strong profunctor
            between virtual double categories. The neatest way to define all of these notions at once is to use the general framework of
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/generalized+multicategories"} generalized multicategories
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi fc
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} fc
            on the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/virtual+equipment"} virtual equipment
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Span
                  %mo =
                  %mi Span
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi Set
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Span = Span(Set)
            we can construct a new virtual equipment
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi vDblProf
                  %mo =
                  %mi KMod
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi Span
                  %mo ,
                  %mi fc
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} vDblProf = KMod(Span,fc)
            whose objects are virtual double categories, whose arrows are functors between them, whose proarrows are profunctors between them, and whose cells are transformations. But we can also give explicit definitions of all of these notions.
          %h3#functors_and_transformations Functors and transformations
          %p
            A
            %strong functor
            of virtual double categories is fairly obvious; it takes each kind of morphism/cell to the same kind, preserving sources, targets, composition, and identities.
          %p
            The relevant
            %strong transformations
            are a “virtual” version of
            %span.newWikiWord
              tight transformations
              %a{:href => "/nlab/new/tight+transformations"} ?
            between ordinary double categories. Specifically, a transformation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi α
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mi F
                  %mo →
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \alpha\colon F\to G
            has a tight arrow component
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi α
                    %mi X
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mi F
                  %mi X
                  %mo →
                  %mi G
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \alpha_X\colon F X\to G X
            for each object
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            of the domain, and a cell component
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi F
                          %mi X
                        %mtd
                          %mover
                            %mo →
                            %mrow
                              %mi F
                              %mi p
                        %mtd
                          %mi F
                          %mi Y
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %msup
                            %mo
                            %mrow
                              %msub
                                %mi α
                                %mi X
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                        %mtd
                          %msup
                            %mo ⇓
                            %mrow
                              %msub
                                %mi α
                                %mi p
                        %mtd
                          %msup
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                            %mrow
                              %msub
                                %mi α
                                %mi Y
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi G
                          %mi X
                        %mtd
                          %munder
                            %mo →
                            %mrow
                              %mi G
                              %mi p
                        %mtd
                          %mi G
                          %mi Y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\array{F X &amp; \overset{F p}{\to} &amp; F Y\\
                  ^{\alpha_X}\downarrow &amp; \Downarrow ^{\alpha_p}&amp; \downarrow^{\alpha_Y}\\
                  G X&amp; \underset{G p}{\to} &amp; G Y}
          %p
            for each loose arrow
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi p
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mi X
                  %mo →
                  %mi Y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p\colon X\to Y
            in the domain. These must be natural with respect to tight composition of arrows and of 2-cells, where we must of course allow composites with arbitrary arities in the latter case.
          %p
            Virtual double categories, functors, and transformations form a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/strict+2-category"} strict 2-category
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monad"} monad
            %em on
            a virtual double category, which is the starting point for one theory of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/generalized+multicategories"} generalized multicategories
          %h3#profunctors Profunctors
          %p
            The
            %strong profunctors
            between virtual double categories are a similar “virtualization” of the notion of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/double+profunctor"} double profunctor
            between double categories. Explicitly, a profunctor
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi H
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mi C
                  %mi ⇸
                  %mi D
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H\colon C &amp;#8696; D
            consists of:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                An ordinary
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/profunctor"} profunctor
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi H
                        %mn 0
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                      %msub
                        %mi C
                        %mn 0
                      %mi ⇸
                      %msub
                        %mi D
                        %mn 0
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H_0\colon C_0 &amp;#8696; D_0
                between the categories of objects and tight arrows.
            %li
              %p
                For each string of loose arrows
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi X
                        %mn 0
                      %mover
                        %mo →
                        %mrow
                          %msub
                            %mi p
                            %mn 1
                      %msub
                        %mi X
                        %mn 1
                      %mo →
                      %mi …
                      %mo →
                      %msub
                        %mi X
                        %mi n
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X_0 \overset{p_1}{\to} X_1 \to\dots \to X_n
                in
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi D
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi Y
                        %mn 0
                      %mover
                        %mo →
                        %mi q
                      %msub
                        %mi Y
                        %mn 1
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y_0 \overset{q}{\to} Y_1
                in
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi C
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi f
                      %mo ∈
                      %msub
                        %mi H
                        %mn 0
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msub
                        %mi X
                        %mn 0
                      %mo ,
                      %msub
                        %mi Y
                        %mn 0
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f \in H_0 (X_0,Y_0)
                and
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi g
                        %mo ∈
                        %msub
                          %mi H
                          %mn 0
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %msub
                          %mi X
                          %mi n
                        %mo ,
                        %msub
                          %mi Y
                          %mn 1
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g\in H_0(X_n,Y_1)
              %p
                %img{:alt => "A cell in a virtual double category", :src => "/nlab/files/virtual-double-category-cell.png"}/
            %li
              %p
                The hetero-cells are acted on by the 2-cells of
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi D
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
                on the top, and by the 2-cells of
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi C
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
                on the bottom, in an evident way, respecting the given action of tight arrows of
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi D
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
                and
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi C
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
                on the elements of
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi H
                          %mn 0
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H_0
          %p
            Every
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/double+profunctor"} double profunctor
            induces such a profunctor in an evident way, but even if
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi D
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
            are (non-virtual) double categories, not every “virtual double profunctor” from
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            to
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi D
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
            need be a double functor; only those for which the “hetero-cells” also factor uniquely through the opcartesian cells in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi D
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
            which make it “representable.”
          %p
            As mentioned above in the context of the abstract definition, virtual double categories, functors, transformations, and profunctors form another virtual double category, which is in fact a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/virtual+equipment"} virtual equipment
          %h3#Monads Monads on virtual double categories
          .un_defn
            %h6#definition_2 Definition
            %p
              A
              %em monad on a virtual double category
              is a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monad"} monad
              in the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-category"} 2-category
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vDbl"} vDbl
          %p
            So a monad on a
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi vDbl
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X \in vDbl
            consists of a functor
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi T
                  %mo :
                  %mi 𝕏
                  %mo →
                  %mi 𝕏
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  T : \mathbb{X} \to \mathbb{X}
          %p
            and transformations
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi η
                  %mo :
                  %mi Id
                  %mo →
                  %mi T
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \eta : Id  \to T
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi μ
                  %mo :
                  %mi T
                  %mi T
                  %mo →
                  %mi T
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mu : T T \to T
            satisfying
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/associativity"} associativity
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/unitality"} unitality
          %h4#monoids_and_modules Monoids and modules
          .un_defn
            %h6#definition_3 Definition
            %p
              For
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi T
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
              a monad on
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝕏
                    %mo ∈
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{X} \in
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vDbl"} vDbl
              %strong
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi T
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
                \-monoid
              is
            %ul
              %li
                %p
                  an object
                  = succeed ";" do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %msub
                            %mi X
                            %mn 0
                          %mo ∈
                          %mi 𝕏
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X_0 \in \mathbb{X}
              %li
                %p
                  a loose morphism
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi X
                          %mn 0
                        %mover
                          %mi ⇸
                          %mi X
                        %mi T
                        %msub
                          %mi X
                          %mn 0
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X_0 \stackrel{X}{&amp;#8696;} T X_0
              %li
                %p
                  an
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/action"} action
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-morphism"} 2-morphism
                .maruku-equation
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mrow
                          %mtable
                            %mtr
                              %mtd
                                %msub
                                  %mi X
                                  %mn 0
                              %mtd
                                %mover
                                  %mi ⇸
                                  %mi X
                              %mtd
                                %mi T
                                %msub
                                  %mi X
                                  %mn 0
                              %mtd
                                %mover
                                  %mi ⇸
                                  %mrow
                                    %mi T
                                    %mi X
                                %msup
                                  %mi T
                                  %mn 2
                              %mtd
                                %msub
                                  %mi X
                                  %mn 0
                            %mtr
                              %mtd
                                %msup
                                  %mrow
                                  %mpadded{:lspace => "-100%width", :width => "0"}
                                    %mo =
                                %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                              %mtd
                              %mtd
                                %msup
                                  %mo ⇓
                                  %mover
                                    %mi x
                                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ¯
                              %mtd
                              %mtd
                                %msup
                                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                                  %mpadded{:width => "0"}
                                    %mi μ
                            %mtr
                              %mtd
                                %msub
                                  %mi X
                                  %mn 0
                              %mtd
                              %mtd
                                %munder
                                  %mi ⇸
                                  %mi X
                              %mtd
                              %mtd
                                %mi T
                                %msub
                                  %mi X
                                  %mn 0
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                        \\array{
                        X_0 &amp;\stackrel{X}{&amp;#8696;} &amp; T X_0
                        \&amp; \stackrel{T X}{&amp;#8696;} T^2 &amp;  X_0
                        \\\
                        {}^{\mathllap{=}}\downarrow &amp;&amp; \Downarrow^{\bar x}
                        \&amp;&amp;  \downarrow^{\mathrlap{\mu}}
                        \\\
                        X_0 &amp;&amp; \underset{X}{&amp;#8696;}  &amp;&amp; T X_0
                        }
                %p
                  and a
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/unit"} unit
                  2-morphism
                .maruku-equation
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mrow
                          %mtable
                            %mtr
                              %mtd
                              %mtd
                              %mtd
                                %msub
                                  %mi X
                                  %mn 0
                            %mtr
                              %mtd
                              %mtd
                                %msup
                                  %mrow
                                  %mpadded{:lspace => "-100%width", :width => "0"}
                                    %mo =
                                %mo ↗
                              %mtd
                                %msup
                                  %mo ⇓
                                  %mover
                                    %mi x
                                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ¯
                              %mtd
                                %msup
                                  %mo ↘
                                  %mpadded{:width => "0"}
                                    %mi η
                            %mtr
                              %mtd
                                %msub
                                  %mi X
                                  %mn 0
                              %mtd
                              %mtd
                                %munder
                                  %mi ⇸
                                  %mi X
                              %mtd
                              %mtd
                                %mi T
                                %msub
                                  %mi X
                                  %mn 0
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                        \\array{
                        \&amp;&amp; X_0
                        \\\
                        \&amp; {}^{\mathllap{=}}\nearrow &amp;\Downarrow^{\bar x}&amp; \searrow^{\mathrlap{\eta}}
                        \\\
                        X_0 &amp;&amp;\underset{X}{&amp;#8696;}&amp;&amp; T X_0
                        }
            %p satisfying the evident compatibility conditions.
          %p
            This is (
            %a{:href => "#CruttwellShulman"}> CruttwellShulman, def. 4.2
            ).
          .un_defn
            %h6#definition_4 Definition
            %p
              A
              = succeed "-monoid" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi T
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi X
                      %mn 0
                    %mover
                      %mi ⇸
                      %mi X
                    %mi T
                    %msub
                      %mi X
                      %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X_0 \stackrel{X}{&amp;#8696;} T X_0
              is called
              %strong normalized
              if its unit 2-morphism
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mrow
                      %mtable
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %msub
                              %mi X
                              %mn 0
                          %mtd
                            %mover
                              %mo →
                              %mrow
                                %msub
                                  %mi U
                                  %mrow
                                    %msub
                                      %mi X
                                      %mn 0
                          %mtd
                            %msub
                              %mi X
                              %mn 0
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %msup
                              %mrow
                              %mpadded{:lspace => "-100%width", :width => "0"}
                                %mo =
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %msup
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                              %mpadded{:width => "0"}
                                %mi η
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %msub
                              %mi X
                              %mn 0
                          %mtd
                            %munder
                              %mi ⇸
                              %mi X
                          %mtd
                            %mi T
                            %msub
                              %mi X
                              %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\array{
                    X_0 &amp;\stackrel{U_{X_0}}{\to}&amp; X_0
                    \\\
                    {}^{\mathllap{=}}\downarrow &amp;&amp; \downarrow^{\mathrlap{\eta}}
                    \\\
                    X_0 &amp;\underset{X}{&amp;#8696;}&amp; T X_0
                    }
            %p
              is a
              = succeed "." do
                %a{:href => "#..."} cartesian 2-morphism
          %h4#generalized_multicategories Generalized multicategories
          .un_defn
            %h6#definition_5 Definition
            %p
              A
              %strong
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/generalized+multicategory"} generalized multicategory
              is a normalized
              = succeed "-monoid" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi T
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi T
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
              on a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/virtual+equipment"} virtual equipment
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝕏
                    %mo ∈
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{X} \in
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vDbl"} vDbl
          %p
            This is (
            %a{:href => "#CruttwellShulman"}> CruttwellShulman, page 7
            ).
          %h2#properties Properties
          %ul
            %li
              The 2-category of virtual double categories is the 2-category of algebras for a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/lax-idempotent+2-comonad"} lax-idempotent 2-comonad
              on the 2-category of (strict)
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/double+categories"} double categories
              and strict double functors, specifically that induced by the inclusion of the 2-category of double categories and strict double functors into the 2-category of double categories and lax double functors: see §2 of
              = succeed "." do
                %a{:href => "#DPP06"} DPP06
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/multicategories"} multicategories
              and
              = succeed ".)" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoidal+categories"} monoidal categories
              %strong normal
              virtual double categories, i.e. those with identity
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/loose+morphisms"} loose morphisms
          %h2#enriching_categories Enriching categories
          %p
            Virtual double categories can be viewed as “the natural place in which to enrich categories.” Specifically, for any set
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi A
                    %mi ch
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A_{ch}
            which has
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            as its objects, only identity tight arrows, exactly one loose arrow from every object to every other object, and exactly one 2-cell in every possible niche. For any other virtual double category
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi W
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} W
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi A
                    %mi ch
                  %mo →
                  %mi W
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A_{ch}\to W
            of virtual double categories is the same as a
            = succeed "-enriched" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi W
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} W
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
          %h2#related_pages Related pages
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/double+category"} double category
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/virtual+equipment"} virtual equipment
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/augmented+virtual+double+category"} augmented virtual double category
          %h2#references References
          %p
            Virtual double categories were first considered by
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Albert+Burroni"} Albert Burroni
            under the name
            %strong multicatégorie
            on page 61 of:
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Albert+Burroni"}> Albert Burroni
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi T
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
                  \-catégories (catégories dans un triple)
              = succeed ".3" do
                %strong 12
              = succeed "," do
                %a{:href => "https://eudml.org/doc/91097"} dml:91097
              = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                %a{:href => "http://www.numdam.org/article/CTGDC_1971__12_3_215_0.pdf"} pdf
          %p Later, they were considered in:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Tom+Leinster"}> Tom Leinster
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Higher Operads, Higher Categories
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "http://www.maths.gla.ac.uk/~tl/book.html"} link
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/math/0305049"} arXiv:0305049
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Tom+Leinster"}> Tom Leinster
                ,
                = succeed "-multicategories," do
                  %strong fc
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/math/9903004"} arxiv
                (1999)
          %p
            They are called
            %strong lax double categories
            in:
          %ul
            %li#DPP06
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Robert+Dawson"}> Robert Dawson
              ,
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Robert+Par%C3%A9"} Robert Paré
              and
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dorette+Pronk"} Dorette Pronk
              = succeed "," do
                %em Paths in double categories
              = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                %a{:href => "http://www.tac.mta.ca/tac/volumes/16/18/16-18abs.html"} TAC
          %p
            and the dual concept is called
            %strong oplax double category
            in:
          %ul
            %li#DawsonParePronk10
              R. Dawson,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Robert+Par%C3%A9"} Robert Paré
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dorette+Pronk"} Dorette Pronk
              = succeed "," do
                %em The span construction
              %strong 24
              (2010), No. 13, 302–377,
              %a{:href => "http://www.tac.mta.ca/tac/volumes/24/13/24-13abs.html"} TAC
              %a{:href => "http://www.ams.org/mathscinet-getitem?mr=2720187"} MR2720187
          %p
            The name
            %strong virtual double category
            is introduced in:
          %ul
            %li#CruttwellShulman
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Geoff+Cruttwell"}> Geoff Cruttwell
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Mike+Shulman"} Mike Shulman
              %em A unified framework for generalized multicategories
              \&amp;lbrack;
              %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/0907.2460"}> arXiv:0907.2460
              \&amp;rbrack;
          %p
            On a
            = succeed "-calculus" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+diagram"} string diagram
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/virtual+double+category"} virtual
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/double+categories"} double categories
            with (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/virtual+equipment"}> virtual
            )
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-category+equipped+with+proarrows"} pro-arrow equipments
          %ul
            %li#Myers16
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/David+Jaz+Myers"}> David Jaz Myers
                ,
                %em String Diagrams For Double Categories and (Virtual) Equipments
                \&amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1612.02762"}> arXiv:1612.02762
                \&amp;rbrack;
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/David+Jaz+Myers"}> David Jaz Myers
                ,
                %em String Diagrams for (Virtual) Proarrow Equipments
                (2017) &amp;lbrack;slides:
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "http://www.mat.uc.pt/~ct2017/slides/myers_d.pdf"} pdf
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/files/Myers-StringDiagrams2017.pdf", :title => "pdf"} pdf
          %p Some more recent work
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Nathanael+Arkor"}> Nathanael Arkor
                ,
                %em Virtual double categories: Past, present, and future
                %a{:href => "https://arkor.co/files/Virtual%20double%20categories%20%28SIC%202025%29.pdf"} slides
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Nathanael+Arkor"}> Nathanael Arkor
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Exponentiable virtual double categories and presheaves for double categories
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/2508.11611"} arXiv:2508.11611
                (2025).
          %p
            See also references at
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/virtual+equipment"} virtual equipment
